<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II, Archduke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1540">1540</a>–<a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Charles II of Inner Austria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II, Archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was a younger son of Emperor Ferdinand I who received Inner Austria, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> from 1564. He organised defences against Ottoman raids along the Croatian frontier and invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> to Graz to promote Catholic reform. His son Ferdinand II later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-archduke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Charles II, Archduke of Austria" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé González y Serrano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonz%C3%A1lez_-_Archduke_Charles_of_Austria,_Duke_of_Styria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
